['Here is a simplified example of how you might implement this in Java using Apache Kafka as a stream processing platform and Hazelcast as an in-memory data grid. This code does not include all the details mentioned in your request, but it should give you a good starting point. 

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.*;
import com.hazelcast.core.*;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;

public class StreamProcessor {
    private static final String TOPIC = "input-topic";
    private static final MessageDigest md5Digest;

    static {
        try {
            md5Digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }

    private HazelcastInstance hazelcast;
    private KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer;

    public StreamProcessor() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "test-group");
        props.put("key.deserializer", StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put("value.deserializer", StringDeserializer.class.getName());

        consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props);
        consumer.subscribe(Collections.singletonList(TOPIC));

        hazelcast = Hazelcast.newHazelcastInstance();
    }

    public void process() {
        while (true) {
            ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(100);

            for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
                try {
                    byte[] digest = md5Digest.digest(record.value().getBytes());

                    ISet<byte[]> hashes = hazelcast.getSet("hashes");
                    if (!hashes.add(digest)) {
                        System.out.println("Duplicate hash detected: " + digest);
                    } else {
                        // Store the hash in an external data store or another Hazelcast IMap, etc.
                    }
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    System.err.println("Error processing record: " + record.value());
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new StreamProcessor().process();
    }
}
```
This code uses Hazelcast's distributed data structures to store the MD5 hashes and detect duplicates. It also uses Kafka for stream processing, which allows it to scale horizontally by adding more consumers as needed. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all the details mentioned in your request such as error handling, logging, integration with an external data store, etc. You would need to add these features according to your specific requirements. Also, you may want to use a library like Apache Commons Codec for generating MD5 hashes instead of Java's built-in MessageDigest class.
