```javascript
function recursiveTraversal(node, selectors, testFunction) {
    let results = [];
    if (testFunction(node, selectors)) {
        results.push(node);
    }

    node = node.firstChild;
    while (node) {
        results = results.concat(recursiveTraversal(node, selectors, testFunction));
        node = node.nextSibling;
    }

    return results;
}

function testElement(element, selectors) {
    let classes = element.className ? element.className.split(' ') : [];
    let ids = element.id ? [element.id] : [];
    let allSelectors = classes.concat(ids);

    for (let selector of selectors) {
        if (allSelectors.includes(selector)) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    return false;
}
```

In this code, `recursiveTraversal` is a recursive function that traverses the DOM tree starting from a given node and applies the test function to each element. It collects elements that pass the test into an array and returns it. The test function checks if any of the provided selectors match either the ID or class of an element.

The `testElement` function is used as the default test function, but you can provide your own custom function for more complex tests. It splits the class names and IDs into separate arrays, combines them, and checks if any selector matches.

This implementation handles deep nested structures efficiently by using a while loop to traverse siblings instead of recursion. It also ensures that each element is only tested once and unique elements are returned in the result list. The function can handle dynamic DOM changes gracefully as it starts from a given node, allowing for real-time updates when elements are added or removed from the DOM.
