['Here is an example of how you might implement this task in Java. This code uses Guava's Multiset as the hash map implementation, which is optimized for memory usage and efficient lookups. It also uses a BufferedReader to read the file line by line, which allows it to handle large files that may exceed available RAM.

```java
import com.google.common.collect.HashMultimap;
import com.google.common.collect.Multiset;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.*;

import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class WordCounter {
    private static final Logger logger = LogManager.getLogger(WordCounter.class);

    public void processFile(String filePath) throws IOException {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        Multiset<String> wordCounts = HashMultimap.create().countMap();

        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(new FileInputStream(filePath), StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                long startLineTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                List<String> words = Arrays.stream(line.split("\\s+"))
                        .filter(word -> !word.isEmpty())
                        .collect(Collectors.toList());
                wordCounts.addAll(words);
                long endLineTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                logger.info("Processed line in {} ms", (endLineTime - startLineTime));
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            logger.error("Error processing file", e);
            throw e;
        } finally {
            long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
            logger.info("Total processing time: {} ms", (endTime - startTime));
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses log4j for logging, which is a widely-used library for logging in Java. It logs the number of milliseconds taken to process each line and the total processing time at the end. If an error occurs during file processing, it logs the error and rethrows the exception. This allows the calling code to handle any issues with the file or logging service gracefully.

Please note that this is a basic implementation and may need to be adjusted based on your specific requirements and constraints. For example, you might want to use a different hash map implementation if Guava's Multiset does not meet your performance needs. You might also want to adjust the way lines are split into words depending on how your input file is structured.
']

