["Here is an example of how you might implement this in PHP using a DAO class for interacting with your database. This code assumes that you have a UserDAO class with methods to getUserById, updatePassword, and logPasswordChange. It also uses the Monolog library for logging. 

```php
<?php
require 'vendor/autoload.php'; // assuming you're using composer
use Monolog\Logger;
use Monolog\Handler\StreamHandler;

class UserService {
    private $userDAO;
    private $logger;

    public function __construct(UserDAO $userDAO) {
        $this->userDAO = $userDAO;

        // create a log channel
        $this->logger = new Logger('password_updates');
        $this->logger->pushHandler(new StreamHandler(__DIR__.'/logs/password_changes.log', Logger::INFO));
    }

    public function updatePassword($userId, $currentPassword, $newPassword) {
        // validate password strength
        if (!$this->isStrongEnough($newPassword)) {
            throw new Exception('New password is not strong enough');
        }

        $user = $this->userDAO->getUserById($userId);

        // verify current password
        if (!password_verify($currentPassword, $user['password'])) {
            throw new Exception('Current password is incorrect');
        }

        // check if the password has been used for at least a specified duration
        $lastChangeTime = strtotime($user['password_updated_at']);
        $duration = 30 * 24 * 60 * 60; // 30 days in seconds
        if (time() - $lastChangeTime < $duration) {
            throw new Exception('Password has been used within the last 30 days');
        }

        // handle concurrent updates safely
        $this->userDAO->beginTransaction();
        try {
            $this->userDAO->updatePassword($userId, password_hash($newPassword, PASSWORD_DEFAULT));

            // log the change
            $this->logger->info('User ' . $userId . ' updated their password');

            $this->userDAO->commit();
        } catch (Exception $e) {
            $this->userDAO->rollBack();
            throw $e; // rethrow the exception to be caught by caller
        }
    }

    private function isStrongEnough($password) {
        // implement your own password strength check here
        return strlen($password) >= 8;
    }
}
?>
```
This code uses a transaction to ensure that the update and log operations are atomic, preventing race conditions. It also logs changes to the user's password using Monolog, which is efficient and does not consume excessive disk space. The logging strategy can be adjusted based on your application's expected load and resource constraints. 

Please note that this code assumes you have a UserDAO class with methods for interacting with your database. You would need to implement these methods according to your specific database schema and requirements. Also, the password strength check is very basic and should be replaced with a more robust implementation in a real-world application. 

This code also does not handle error conditions or exceptions that might occur during the update process. In a production environment, you would want to add appropriate error handling and logging for these scenarios.
"]

["Here is a basic implementation in C that meets your requirements. This code does not include any error checking or handling for simplicity. In a real-world scenario, you would want to add appropriate error checks and handle memory allocation failures gracefully. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef struct {
    int state;
    bool is_accepting;
} State;

typedef struct {
    State* states;
    int num_states;
} FSA;

int* processFSA(int* currentStateList, int numStatesInList, FSA* fsa, int lastStateIndex, bool filterAcceptingStates, pthread_mutex_t* lock) {
    // Lock the mutex to ensure thread-safety
    if (pthread_mutex_lock(lock) != 0) {
        return NULL;
    }

    int newNumStates = numStatesInList + fsa->num_states - lastStateIndex;
    // Allocate memory for the updated state list
    int* updatedStateList = (int*)malloc(newNumStates * sizeof(int));
    if (updatedStateList == NULL) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(lock);  // Unlock mutex before returning to prevent deadlocks
        return NULL;
    }

    // Copy the existing state list into the updated state list
    for (int i = 0; i < numStatesInList; ++i) {
        updatedStateList[i] = currentStateList[i];
    }

    // Add new states from the FSA to the updated state list, skipping duplicates and optionally filtering accepting states
    int index = numStatesInList;
    for (int i = 0; i < fsa->num_states; ++i) {
        bool isDuplicate = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < index; ++j) {
            if (updatedStateList[j] == fsa->states[i].state) {
                isDuplicate = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (!isDuplicate && (!filterAcceptingStates || fsa->states[i].is_accepting)) {
            updatedStateList[index++] = fsa->states[i].state;
        }
    }

    // Update the number of states in the list
    numStatesInList = index;

    // Unlock the mutex before returning to allow other threads to access the shared state data structures
    pthread_mutex_unlock(lock);

    return updatedStateList;
}
```
This function assumes that the FSA and current state list are already initialized. It also assumes that the lock is already initialized and passed in as a parameter. The function dynamically allocates memory for the updated state list, which can be freed by the caller when it's no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.
"]

