```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int id;
    time_t timestamp;
    int priority;
} Packet;

typedef struct Node {
    Packet packet;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
Node* tail = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock;
int count = 0;

void processPackets() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    while (head != NULL) {
        Node* temp = head;
        head = head->next;
        if(temp->packet.priority > highestPriority){
            highestPriority = temp->packet.priority;
            highestPriorityPacketId = temp->packet.id;
            highestPriorityTimestamp = temp->packet.timestamp;
        }
        free(temp);
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void* packetHandler(void* arg) {
    Packet* packet = (Packet*)arg;
    Node* newNode = (Node*)malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->packet = *packet;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (tail != NULL) {
        tail->next = newNode;
    } else {
        head = newNode;
    }
    tail = newNode;
    count++;
    if(count >= 10000){
        processPackets();
        count = 0;
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code uses a linked list to handle incoming packets. It creates a new thread for each packet and adds it to the end of the queue. When the queue size reaches 10,000, it starts processing the packets in the queue. The processing involves finding the highest priority packet and freeing up memory used by processed packets.

This code does not handle varying network conditions or latency spikes as these would require more complex handling that is beyond the scope of this simple example. Also, it doesn't account for potential packet loss during high load. To handle this, you could implement a retry mechanism or use a different data structure like a priority queue to ensure that no packet is dropped under high load.

