['Here is a simplified example of how you might structure your Rust program with these requirements in mind. This is just a starting point and would need to be expanded upon for a full implementation. 

```rust
use std::ffi::CString;
use std::os::raw::c_char;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use tokio::task::spawn_blocking;
use tokio::time::delay_for;
use tokio::sync::Mutex as TokioMutex;

extern "C" {
    fn log_error(msg: *const c_char);
}

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Logger {
    buffer: Arc<TokioMutex<Vec<String>>>,
}

impl Logger {
    pub async fn new() -> Self {
        let logger = Self {
            buffer: Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(Vec::new())),
        };

        tokio::spawn(async move {
            loop {
                delay_for(std::time::Duration::from_secs(5)).await;
                logger.flush().await;
            }
        });

        logger
    }

    pub async fn log(&self, msg: String) {
        let mut buffer = self.buffer.lock().await;
        buffer.push(msg);
    }

    pub async fn flush(&self) {
        let mut buffer = self.buffer.lock().await;
        while let Some(msg) = buffer.pop() {
            unsafe {
                let c_str = CString::new(msg).unwrap();
                log_error(c_str.as_ptr());
            }
        }
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let logger = Logger::new().await;

    // Process command-line arguments and execute tasks...
    spawn_blocking(move || {
        unsafe { log_error(CString::new("Started task").unwrap().as_ptr()) };
    })
    .await?;

    Ok(())
}
```

This code creates a `Logger` struct that buffers logs and periodically flushes them to an external service. The `log` function adds new log messages to the buffer, while the `flush` function sends all buffered messages to the C logging function. 

The `main` function initializes the logger and starts processing command-line arguments and executing tasks. It also logs a "Started task" message when it starts each task.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all of the features you requested, such as handling system restarts or evaluating different buffer sizes and flushing intervals on performance and reliability. Also, error handling in this code is minimal and may need to be expanded upon for a production environment. 

Also, please note that FFI calls are inherently unsafe in Rust due to the possibility of memory safety issues. Always ensure you have a good understanding of how your C functions work before using them in Rust.
