impl TaskManager {
    fn new(config: Config) -> Result<Self> {
        let dir_path = config.dir_path.unwrap_or_else(|| env::current_dir()?.into());
        let data_dir_mutex = Mutex::new(None);

        Ok(TaskManager {
            dir_path,
            data_dir_mutex,
        })
    }

    fn create_task(&self) -> Result<PathBuf> {
        let timestamp = Utc::now().timestamp();
        let task_data_dir = self.dir_path.join(format![("task_data_{}", timestamp));

        if fs::metadata(&task_data_dir).is_some() {
            return Err(Error::CreateDirError);
        }

        fs::create_dir(&task_data_dir)?;

        *self.data_dir_mutex.lock().unwrap() = Some(task_data_dir.clone());
        env::set_var("DATA_DIR", &task_data_dir)?;

        Ok(task_data_dir)
    }
}
```
This code creates a `TaskManager` struct that manages the creation of directories and environment variables. It uses a Mutex to ensure thread safety when accessing shared resources. The `create_task` function is used to create a new task directory and set the `DATA_DIR` environment variable. 

Please note that this code does not cover all your requirements, such as logging, error handling, cleanup of resources, setting additional environment variables, integration into a larger application, performance optimization, resilience against varying workloads and system conditions etc. You would need to expand on this basic implementation to meet these requirements.
']

