["Here's a basic implementation in C using pthread library for thread safety. This code does not include all the functionalities you asked for, but it should give you an idea of how to start implementing such a function. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    // Define your structure here
} NFAPI_MESSAGE_PDU_T;

typedef struct node {
    NFAPI_MESSAGE_PDU_T *message;
    struct node *next;
} Node;

Node *head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

void addMessage(NFAPI_MESSAGE_PDU_T *message) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node *newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->message = message;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

NFAPI_MESSAGE_PDU_T *getMessageByUEID(int UEID) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node *temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        if (temp->message->RX_UE_CONTEXT.UEID == UEID) {
            NFAPI_MESSAGE_PDU_T *result = malloc(sizeof(NFAPI_MESSAGE_PDU_T));
            memcpy(result, temp->message, sizeof(NFAPI_MESSAGE_PDU_T));

            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            return result;
        }

        temp = temp->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return NULL;
}
```
This code uses a linked list to store messages. Each node in the list contains a pointer to a message and a link to the next node. The `addMessage` function adds new messages at the head of the list, ensuring quick access for retrieval operations. 

The `getMessageByUEID` function searches through the list from the head to find a message with a matching UEID. If such a message is found, it's copied and returned. 

Both functions are thread-safe by using a mutex lock before accessing or modifying the linked list. This ensures that only one thread can access or modify the list at any given time, preventing race conditions and ensuring data consistency.
