// Helper function to find maximum value in array
int max_value(int* arr, int size) {
    if (size == 0) return -1; // Return -1 for empty array

    int max = arr[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < size; ++i) {
        if (arr[i] > max) max = arr[i];
    }
    return max;
}

// Main function to read and store integers in array
void read_and_store() {
    int* arr = malloc(10000 * sizeof(int)); // Allocate memory for 10,000 integers
    if (arr == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed. Exiting...\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    int count = 0;
    while (count < 10000 && scanf("%d", &arr[count]) == 1) { // Read integers until 10,000 or EOF
        if (arr[count] <= 0) continue; // Skip non-positive values
        ++count;
    }

    int max = max_value(arr, count);
    printf("Maximum value: %d\n", max);

    free(arr); // Free allocated memory
}
```
This code first allocates a block of memory for 10,000 integers. It then reads integers from standard input until it has read 10,000 or reached the end of file (EOF). If an integer is non-positive, it skips over it and continues to the next one. After reading all valid integers, it finds the maximum value using a helper function `max_value` and prints it. Finally, it frees the allocated memory.

The code also includes error handling for memory allocation failures by checking if `malloc` returned NULL. If so, it prints an error message and exits with failure status. This ensures that the program does not crash unexpectedly under stress.
