['Here's a basic implementation of `log_error_condition` using POSIX threads for thread safety, and fopen/fprintf for file operations. This code does not handle concurrent access from multiple processes or read-only directories. It also doesn't include error handling for memory allocation failures. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

static pthread_mutex_t logMutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void log_error_condition(const char* filename, const char* functionName, int lineNumber, const char* format, ...) {
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, format);

    // Get current date and time
    time_t now = time(NULL);
    struct tm *timeinfo = localtime(&now);
    char timestamp[20];
    strftime(timestamp, sizeof(timestamp), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S", timeinfo);

    // Format the message
    int len = snprintf(NULL, 0, format, args) + 1;
    if (len < 0) {
        va_end(args);
        return;
    }
    char *message = malloc(len);
    if (!message) {
        va_end(args);
        return;
    }
    snprintf(message, len, format, args);

    // Log the message to file
    pthread_mutex_lock(&logMutex);
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "a");
    if (file) {
        fprintf(file, "%s [%s:%d] %s\n", timestamp, functionName, lineNumber, message);
        fclose(file);
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&logMutex);

    free(message);
    va_end(args);
}
```
This code uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can log at a time, which is necessary for concurrent access from multiple threads. It also uses `snprintf` with NULL buffer and 0 size to determine the required length of the formatted string without actually allocating memory, then uses `malloc` to allocate exactly enough memory for the formatted string. This approach avoids potential issues with variable argument lists that could cause buffer overflows.

Please note that this is a basic implementation and may need further optimization or additional features depending on your specific requirements. For example, you might want to add error handling for file operations, handle concurrent access from multiple processes, or implement fallback strategies for read-only directories or non-existent files. Also, consider using a more efficient logging library or framework if performance is a concern.
']

