["Here is a basic implementation of such a function in JavaScript using Node.js. This code does not cover all aspects you mentioned, but it provides a starting point for your requirements. 

```javascript
const EventEmitter = require('events');

class DataStreamManager extends EventEmitter {
    constructor(batchSize) {
        super();
        this.dataPoints = [];
        this.uniqueIds = new Set();
        this.isPaused = false;
        this.batchSize = batchSize || 100;
    }

    addDataPoint(id, data) {
        if (this.isPaused || this.dataPoints.length >= this.batchSize) {
            return;
        }

        // Avoid duplicate entries
        if (!this.uniqueIds.has(id)) {
            this.uniqueIds.add(id);
            this.dataPoints.push({ id, data });
        }

        if (this.dataPoints.length === this.batchSize) {
            this.emit('batchReady', this.dataPoints);
            this.dataPoints = [];
            this.uniqueIds.clear();
        }
    }

    pause() {
        this.isPaused = true;
    }

    resume() {
        this.isPaused = false;
    }
}
```
This code creates a `DataStreamManager` class that extends Node's built-in EventEmitter. It maintains an array of data points and a set of unique IDs to avoid duplicates. When the batch size is reached, it emits a 'batchReady' event with the current batch of data points.

The `addDataPoint` method adds new data points to the batch if not paused or if the batch is full. It also handles duplicate entries by checking the unique ID set before adding each point.

The `pause` and `resume` methods control whether new data points are added to the batch. 

This code does not include real-time metrics, error handling strategies for network failures or server downtime, idempotency checks, logging, or any of the other advanced features you mentioned. Implementing these would require a more complex solution and likely involve additional libraries or frameworks.
"]

["Here is an example of how you might implement such a function using JavaScript and MutationObserver API. This code does not include integration with any external library as it's not specified in your task. Also, please note that this is a simplified version and may need to be adjusted based on the specific requirements of your project:

```javascript
class DynamicCollection {
  constructor(selector) {
    this.elements = [];
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.observer = null;

    if (typeof selector === 'string') {
      // If the selector is a string, assume it's a CSS selector
      this.updateElements(document.querySelectorAll(selector));

      this.observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
        for (const mutation of mutations) {
          if (mutation.type === 'childList') {
            // If a child node is added or removed, update the elements
            this.updateElements(document.querySelectorAll(selector));
          }
        }
      });

      // Start observing changes to the DOM
      this.observer.observe(document.body, {childList: true, subtree: true});
    } else if (typeof selector === 'function') {
      // If the selector is a function, assume it's a tag name matcher
      this.updateElements([...document.getElementsByTagName(selector)]);

      this.observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
        for (const mutation of mutations) {
          if (mutation.type === 'childList') {
            // If a child node is added or removed, update the elements
            this.updateElements([...document.getElementsByTagName(selector)]);
          }
        }
      });

      // Start observing changes to the DOM
      this.observer.observe(document.body, {childList: true, subtree: true});
    } else {
      throw new Error('Invalid selector type');
    }
  }

  updateElements(newElements) {
    // Clear the cache when elements are updated
    this.cache.clear();

    // Update the elements array with the new elements
    this.elements = Array.from(newElements);
  }

  getElementByIndex(index) {
    if (this.cache.has(index)) {
      return this.cache.get(index);
    } else {
      const element = this.elements[index];

      // Cache the result for future use
      this.cache.set(index, element);

      return element;
    }
  }

  dispose() {
    if (this.observer) {
      this.observer.disconnect();
      this.observer = null;
    }

    // Clear the elements and cache arrays to free up memory
    this.elements.length = 0;
    this.cache.clear();
  }
}
```
This code creates a `DynamicCollection` class that manages a collection of DOM elements matching a specified selector. It uses the MutationObserver API to continuously update the collection in real-time as new elements are added or removed from the DOM. The caching mechanism is implemented using a Map, which allows for efficient lookups but may consume more memory if the number of unique indices is large.
