["Here is a basic implementation of such a system using JavaScript's Promise API and ES6 modules. This code does not handle circular dependencies or self-references, as these would require a more complex dependency graph management system which is beyond the scope of this task. 

```javascript
class ScriptLoader {
    constructor() {
        this.loadedScripts = new Set();
        this.loadingPromises = {};
    }

    load(scriptUrl) {
        if (this.loadedScripts.has(scriptUrl)) {
            return Promise.resolve();
        }

        if (!this.loadingPromises[scriptUrl]) {
            this.loadingPromises[scriptUrl] = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
                const scriptElement = document.createElement('script');
                scriptElement.src = scriptUrl;
                scriptElement.onload = () => {
                    this.loadedScripts.add(scriptUrl);
                    resolve();
                };
                scriptElement.onerror = (err) => reject(new Error(`Failed to load ${scriptUrl}: ${err}`));
                document.head.appendChild(scriptElement);
            });
        }

        return this.loadingPromises[scriptUrl];
    }
}
```

This script loader class maintains a set of loaded scripts and a map of loading promises for each script URL. When `load` is called with a script URL, it checks if the script has already been loaded. If not, it creates a new promise that resolves when the script loads successfully or rejects on error. The script element is added to the document head asynchronously and its load event triggers the promise resolution.

This implementation assumes that scripts are idempotent (i.e., they can be loaded multiple times without causing problems), which may not always be the case in a real-world application. It also does not handle cases where scripts contain self-references or circular dependencies, as these would require more complex dependency graph management and error handling.
"]

