['Here is an example of how you might implement such a method in Java using NIO (New I/O) for non-blocking I/O operations, CompletableFuture for asynchronous processing, and SLF4J for logging. This code does not include the hardware acceleration module integration or plugin mechanism as those would require more specific details about the external modules and plugins to be implemented.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class ConnectionManager {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ConnectionManager.class);
    private Selector selector;
    private SocketChannel socketChannel;

    public void establishConnection(String hostname, int port) throws IOException {
        this.selector = Selector.open();
        this.socketChannel = SocketChannel.open(new InetSocketAddress(hostname, port));
        this.socketChannel.configureBlocking(false);
        this.socketChannel.register(this.selector, SelectionKey.OP_CONNECT | SelectionKey.OP_READ | SelectionKey.OP_WRITE);

        CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
            try {
                while (true) {
                    if (this.selector.selectNow() > 0) {
                        Set<SelectionKey> selectedKeys = this.selector.selectedKeys();
                        Iterator<SelectionKey> keyIterator = selectedKeys.iterator();

                        while (keyIterator.hasNext()) {
                            SelectionKey key = keyIterator.next();

                            if (key.isConnectable()) {
                                logger.info("Connection established");
                                // Handle connection state changes here
                            } else if (key.isReadable()) {
                                ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocate(1024);
                                this.socketChannel.read(buffer);
                                buffer.flip();

                                logger.info("Received data: " + new String(buffer.array()));
                            } else if (key.isWritable()) {
                                ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.wrap("Hello, server!".getBytes());
                                this.socketChannel.write(buffer);

                                logger.info("Sent data");
                            }

                            keyIterator.remove();
                        }
                    }
                }
            } catch (IOException e) {
                logger.error("Connection error", e);
            }
        });
    }
}
```
This code establishes a connection to a remote address using Java NIO, handles the connection state changes asynchronously, and logs both successful connections and received data. It also includes basic error handling for connection errors. 

Please note that this is just an example and does not include all of the features you requested such as hardware acceleration module integration or plugin mechanism. You would need to implement those based on your specific requirements and constraints.
']

['This task requires a deep understanding of Android development, particularly in terms of managing resources efficiently, handling edge cases, integrating with background services, optimizing for large PNG files, monitoring performance, enabling dynamic theme switching, supporting different orientations, automating cache updates, and implementing a clear mechanism. 

Here is a high-level overview of how you might approach this task:

```java
public class ThemeManager {
    private static final String DEFAULT_THEME_DIR = "defaultThemeDir";
    private static final int MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 10 * 1024 * 1024; // 10MB

    private LruCache<String, Bitmap> mMemoryCache;
    private DiskLruCache mDiskCache;
    private ExecutorService mExecutor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
    private String mThemeDirPath;

    public ThemeManager(Context context) {
        File cacheDir = new File(context.getCacheDir(), "theme_cache");
        mDiskCache = DiskLruCache.open(cacheDir, 1, 1, MAX_CACHE_SIZE);

        int maxMemory = (int) (Runtime.getRuntime().maxMemory() / 1024);
        int cacheSize = maxMemory / 8;
        mMemoryCache = new LruCache<String, Bitmap>(cacheSize) {
            @Override
            protected int sizeOf(String key, Bitmap bitmap) {
                return bitmap.getByteCount() / 1024;
            }
        };

        mThemeDirPath = System.getenv().getOrDefault(DEFAULT_THEME_DIR, "default");
    }

    public void loadBitmap(final String resId, final ImageView imageView) {
        Bitmap bitmap = getBitmapFromMemCache(resId);
        if (bitmap == null) {
            mExecutor.execute(() -> {
                FileInputStream in = null;
                try {
                    in = new FileInputStream(new File(mThemeDirPath, resId));
                    bitmap = BitmapFactory.decodeStream(in);
                    addBitmapToCache(resId, bitmap);
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                } finally {
                    if (in != null) {
                        try {
                            in.close();
                        } catch (IOException e) {
                            e.printStackTrace();
                        }
                    }
                }

                new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper()).post(() -> {
                    if (imageView != null) {
                        imageView.setImageBitmap(bitmap);
                    }
                });
            });
        } else {
            imageView.setImageBitmap(bitmap);
        }
    }

    private void addBitmapToCache(String resId, Bitmap bitmap) {
        if (getBitmapFromMemCache(resId) == null) {
            mMemoryCache.put(resId, bitmap);
        }

        try {
            DiskLruCache.Editor editor = mDiskCache.edit(resId);
            OutputStream out = new BufferedOutputStream(editor.newOutputStream(0), 8 * 1024);
            bitmap.compress(Bitmap.CompressFormat.PNG, 100, out);
            editor.commit();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private Bitmap getBitmapFromMemCache(String resId) {
        return mMemoryCache.get(resId);
    }
}
```
This is a simplified example and does not cover all the points mentioned in your task. For instance, it doesn't handle fallback themes or user-driven theme switching. Also, it assumes that all resources are PNG files which might not be the case for all applications. 

For a complete solution, you would need to implement additional features like error handling, edge cases, dynamic theme switching, and so on. It's also recommended to use well-maintained third-party libraries for file handling and image processing to ensure long-term maintainability and performance.
