['Here's a thread-safe C function that manages access to a shared resource in a high-traffic server application. This code uses POSIX semaphores for synchronization and error handling. It also includes mechanisms to handle potential errors gracefully, ensuring the semaphore is deleted only if it was successfully initialized:

```c
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <stdio.h>

// Function to manage access to a shared resource
void manage_access(int expectedUserLoad) {
    sem_t *sem = NULL;

    // Attempt to initialize the semaphore with an appropriate initial value
    if ((sem = sem_open("mysem", O_CREAT | O_EXCL, 0644, expectedUserLoad)) == SEM_FAILED) {
        if (errno == EEXIST) {
            // If the semaphore already exists, open it in read-only mode
            sem = sem_open("mysem", O_RDONLY);

            if (sem == SEM_FAILED) {
                perror("Failed to open existing semaphore");
                return;
            }
        } else {
            // Handle other error cases
            perror("Failed to create new semaphore");
            return;
        }
    }

    // Use the semaphore...
    if (sem_wait(sem) == -1) {
        perror("Failed to acquire semaphore");
        return;
    }

    // Perform operations on shared resource here...

    if (sem_post(sem) == -1) {
        perror("Failed to release semaphore");
        return;
    }

    // Close the semaphore only if it was successfully opened
    if (sem_close(sem) == -1) {
        perror("Failed to close semaphore");
        return;
    }
}
```
This code is designed to minimize contention and maximize performance by using a semaphore with an initial value that's expected to be the maximum number of concurrent users. However, it's important to note that this approach may lead to resource exhaustion if not properly managed. Therefore, it's crucial to evaluate the impact of different semaphore initial values on system performance and resource contention.
