```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory;

public class HashService
{
    private readonly IMemoryCache _cache;
    private readonly MemoryCacheEntryOptions _cacheOptions;

    public HashService()
    {
        // Set up the cache with a 1 MB size limit and an expiration time of 5 minutes.
        var options = new MemoryDistributedCacheOptions
        {
            SizeLimit = 1024 * 1024 // 1MB
        };

        _cache = new MemoryCache(options);

        _cacheOptions = new MemoryCacheEntryOptions()
            .SetSize(1) // Each entry is assumed to be 1 byte. Adjust as needed.
            .SetSlidingExpiration(TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));
    }

    public string GetHash(string input)
    {
        if (!_cache.TryGetValue(input, out string hash))
        {
            using (var md5 = MD5.Create())
            {
                byte[] inputBytes = System.Text.Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(input);
                byte[] hashBytes = md5.ComputeHash(inputBytes);

                hash = BitConverter.ToString(hashBytes).Replace("-", "").ToLower();

                _cache.Set(input, hash, _cacheOptions);
            }
        }

        return hash;
    }

    public void ClearCache()
    {
        _cache.Compact(1.0); // Compact the cache to remove expired items and release memory.
    }
}
```
This code does not include any external data source interaction or asynchronous processing, but it provides a starting point for building a more complete solution that meets your requirements. It's also worth noting that this is a simple example and real-world usage would require additional considerations such as error handling, logging, and potentially using a distributed cache if you need to scale across multiple servers.
']

['Here is a basic implementation of your requirements in C# using AesCryptoServiceProvider from System.Security.Cryptography namespace. This example does not include the external decryption service integration, as this would require additional details about how it works. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

public class AesDecrypter
{
    private readonly byte[] _key;
    private readonly byte[] _iv;
    private readonly string _hash;

    public AesDecrypter(byte[] key, byte[] iv, string hash)
    {
        _key = key;
        _iv = iv;
        _hash = hash;
    }

    public byte[] DecryptCiphertext(byte[] cipherText)
    {
        try
        {
            using (var aesAlg = new AesCryptoServiceProvider())
            {
                aesAlg.Key = _key;
                aesAlg.IV = _iv;
                aesAlg.Padding = PaddingMode.PKCS7;

                var decryptor = aesAlg.CreateDecryptor(aesAlg.Key, aesAlg.IV);

                using (var msDecrypt = new MemoryStream())
                {
                    using (var csDecrypt = new CryptoStream(msDecrypt, decryptor, CryptoStreamMode.Write))
                    {
                        csDecrypt.Write(cipherText, 0, cipherText.Length);
                    }

                    var plaintextBytes = msDecrypt.ToArray();

                    // Verify the integrity of the decrypted data by comparing it against a precomputed hash value.
                    if (VerifyHash(plaintextBytes))
                    {
                        return plaintextBytes;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        catch (CryptographicException e)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("A Cryptographic error occurred: {0}", e.Message);
        }

        // Return an empty byte array if the integrity check fails or any other exception occurs.
        return new byte[0];
    }

    private bool VerifyHash(byte[] data)
    {
        using (var sha256 = SHA256.Create())
        {
            var hashBytes = sha256.ComputeHash(data);
            return _hash == BitConverter.ToString(hashBytes).Replace("-", "").ToLower();
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a class `AesDecrypter` that takes the encryption key, initialization vector (IV), and hash value as constructor parameters. The `DecryptCiphertext` method decrypts the ciphertext using AES-CBC with the provided key, IV, and padding. It then verifies the integrity of the decrypted data by comparing it against a precomputed hash value. If the verification fails or any exception occurs during decryption, an empty byte array is returned.

Please note that this code does not include error handling for network latency and potential service unavailability when fetching keys and IVs from the external service. This would require additional details about how these services work and could significantly increase the complexity of the code. 

Also, please remember to clear sensitive data (like encryption key and IV) after use to prevent memory dump attacks. In this example, we assume that the `_key` and `_iv` byte arrays are cleared from memory post-decryption.
