["Here's a basic implementation of such a function using C with pthread library for thread safety. This code does not include error handling or validation as it would be quite complex and depend on your specific requirements. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    // Add your structure fields here
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void add(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    memcpy(&newNode->data, &value, sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void update(int rnti, NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        if (temp->data.RNTI == rnti) {
            memcpy(&temp->data, &value, sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
            break;
        }
        temp = temp->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void removeByRnti(int rnti) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* temp = head;
    Node* prev = NULL;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        if (temp->data.RNTI == rnti) {
            if (prev == NULL) {
                head = temp->next;
            } else {
                prev->next = temp->next;
            }
            free(temp);
            break;
        }
        prev = temp;
        temp = temp->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code uses a linked list to store the structures. It's not optimized for performance, but it should be efficient enough for your requirements. The `add` function adds new nodes at the beginning of the list, which is efficient because it doesn't require traversing the entire list. The `update` and `removeByRnti` functions use a simple linear search to find the node with the matching RNTI.

The code uses a mutex for thread safety. This ensures that only one thread can access the linked list at a time, preventing data corruption from simultaneous modifications. However, this also means that only one operation can be performed on the list at a time, which could limit performance under high load. To improve performance under high load, you might want to consider using a more efficient data structure or algorithm, such as a hash table or balanced search tree.
"]

