['Here is a basic implementation of `updateResourceBundle` in C++ using standard library features. This code does not include concurrency control mechanisms such as mutexes or locks to handle multiple threads accessing the resource bundle simultaneously, which would be necessary for a production-level application. 

```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

enum class ErrorCode {
    SUCCESS = 0,
    FILE_OPEN_ERROR,
    EMPTY_FILE,
    TARGET_NOT_FOUND,
    WRITE_FAILURE
};

ErrorCode updateResourceBundle(std::forward_list<std::string>& bundle, const std::string& target, 
                               const std::string& additionalString, const std::string& filePath) {

    // Open the file for reading.
    std::ifstream inFile(filePath);
    if (!inFile) {
        return ErrorCode::FILE_OPEN_ERROR;
    }

    // Read the contents of the file into a forward list.
    std::forward_list<std::string> updatedBundle((std::istream_iterator<std::string>(inFile)), 
                                                 std::istream_iterator<std::string>());

    inFile.close();

    // If the file was empty, add the additional string and return.
    if (updatedBundle.empty()) {
        updatedBundle.push_front(additionalString);
        bundle = std::move(updatedBundle);
        return ErrorCode::SUCCESS;
    }

    // Find the target string in the forward list.
    auto it = std::find(updatedBundle.begin(), updatedBundle.end(), target);
    if (it == updatedBundle.end()) {
        return ErrorCode::TARGET_NOT_FOUND;
    }

    // Insert or append based on the position of the target string.
    *(++it) = additionalString;

    // Open the file for writing.
    std::ofstream outFile(filePath);
    if (!outFile) {
        return ErrorCode::FILE_OPEN_ERROR;
    }

    // Write the updated forward list back to the file.
    std::copy(updatedBundle.begin(), updatedBundle.end(), 
              std::ostream_iterator<std::string>(outFile, " "));

    outFile.close();

    if (!outFile) {
        return ErrorCode::WRITE_FAILURE;
    }

    bundle = std::move(updatedBundle);
    return ErrorCode::SUCCESS;
}
```
This code assumes that the resource bundle is a list of strings, each string being separated by whitespace. It also assumes that the target string and additional string are single words. If these assumptions do not hold for your use case, you will need to adjust this code accordingly. 

Please note that error handling in this function is very basic and does not provide detailed information about what exactly went wrong. In a production-level application, you would want to include more robust error handling mechanisms.
']

['Here is a basic implementation of your requirements using Apache HttpClient library in Java. This example does not cover all aspects you mentioned due to complexity but it gives you a good starting point. 

```java
import org.apache.http.HttpEntity;
import org.apache.http.client.config.RequestConfig;
import org.apache.http.client.methods.*;
import org.apache.http.entity.StringEntity;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.CloseableHttpClient;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.HttpClients;
import org.apache.http.util.EntityUtils;

public class HttpClientExample {
    private CloseableHttpClient httpClient;

    public HttpClientExample() {
        RequestConfig requestConfig = RequestConfig.custom()
                .setSocketTimeout(5000) // timeout in milliseconds
                .setConnectTimeout(5000) // timeout in milliseconds
                .build();

        httpClient = HttpClients.custom()
                .setDefaultRequestConfig(requestConfig)
                .setMaxConnPerRoute(10) // maximum connections per route
                .setMaxConnTotal(20) // total maximum connections
                .build();
    }

    public String get(String url) throws Exception {
        HttpGet httpGet = new HttpGet(url);
        return executeRequest(httpGet);
    }

    public String post(String url, String body) throws Exception {
        HttpPost httpPost = new HttpPost(url);
        httpPost.setEntity(new StringEntity(body));
        return executeRequest(httpPost);
    }

    // Similar methods for PUT, DELETE and PATCH can be implemented here...

    private String executeRequest(HttpUriRequest request) throws Exception {
        CloseableHttpResponse response = httpClient.execute(request);

        try {
            HttpEntity entity = response.getEntity();

            if (entity != null) {
                return EntityUtils.toString(entity);
            } else {
                throw new Exception("No response received");
            }
        } finally {
            response.close();
        }
    }
}
```
This is a very basic example and does not include error handling, logging, configuration file integration or performance metrics collection. For these features you would need to add additional code and possibly use other libraries such as SLF4J for logging, Apache Commons Configuration for reading the configuration file, etc. 

