['Here is a basic implementation of your requirements in JavaScript using recursion and memoization techniques. This code does not include the integration with third-party services as it would require additional libraries or APIs, but it provides a good starting point for you to build upon. 

```javascript
const cache = {};
let shouldCache = true;

function getElements(selector) {
    if (shouldCache && cache[selector]) return cache[selector];

    const elements = [];
    const walker = document.createTreeWalker(document.body, NodeFilter.SHOW_ELEMENT);
    let node;

    while (node = walker.nextNode()) {
        if (node.matches && node.matches(selector)) elements.push(node);
    }

    if (shouldCache) cache[selector] = elements;
    return elements;
}

function invalidateCache() {
    shouldCache = false;
    for (const key in cache) delete cache[key];
    shouldCache = true;
}
```

This code uses a TreeWalker to traverse the DOM tree, which is more efficient than recursion and can handle deeply nested structures. It also includes basic caching using an object where each property corresponds to a selector and its value is an array of matching elements. 

The `invalidateCache` function disables caching by setting `shouldCache` to false, then clears the cache object, and finally re-enables caching. This ensures that any subsequent calls to `getElements` will not use a cached result but instead traverse the DOM tree again. 

Please note that this is a basic implementation and does not include error handling or optimizations for edge cases such as malformed HTML structures, high latency networks, or dynamic updates. You would need to add these features according to your specific requirements. Also, integrating with third-party services would require additional libraries or APIs, which are beyond the scope of this question.
