["Here is a simplified example of how you might implement this using JavaScript's `fetch` API for network requests and promises to manage asynchronous operations. This code does not include the logic for caching, file versioning or conflict resolution due to complexity and size constraints. 

```javascript
class FileLoader {
    constructor(apiUrl) {
        this.loadedFiles = {};
        this.apiUrl = apiUrl;
    }

    loadFile(fileName) {
        if (this.loadedFiles[fileName]) {
            return Promise.resolve();
        }

        return fetch(`${this.apiUrl}/metadata/${fileName}`)
            .then(response => response.json())
            .catch(() => { throw new Error(`Failed to load metadata for ${fileName}.`); })
            .then(({ dependencies, url }) => {
                const dependencyPromises = dependencies.map(dependency => this.loadFile(dependency));
                return Promise.all(dependencyPromises)
                    .then(() => fetch(url))
                    .catch(() => { throw new Error(`Failed to load ${fileName}.`); })
                    .then(response => response.text())
                    .then((code) => {
                        try {
                            eval(code); // eslint-disable-line no-eval
                            this.loadedFiles[fileName] = true;
                        } catch (error) {
                            throw new Error(`Failed to execute ${fileName}.`);
                        }
                    });
            });
    }
}
```
This code creates a `FileLoader` class that manages the loading of JavaScript files. It uses the fetch API to retrieve metadata about each file, including its dependencies and URL for downloading the actual code. The `loadFile` method recursively loads all dependencies before attempting to load and execute the requested file. If any operation fails, it throws an error with a meaningful message.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include caching or conflict resolution logic. Also, using eval() can be dangerous if used on untrusted code as it can lead to security vulnerabilities. It's recommended to use Webpack or other bundlers for managing dependencies in production applications.
