["Here is a basic implementation in C using pthreads for thread safety and dynamic memory allocation. This code does not include any logging or configuration libraries as those would be specific to your project. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

#define MAX_BUFFERS 10000

typedef struct {
    int bufferID;
    time_t timestamp;
    int statusFlag;
} RX_BUFFER;

RX_BUFFER* buffers[MAX_BUFFERS];
int numBuffers = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void initializeBuffer(RX_BUFFER* buffer) {
    buffer->bufferID = numBuffers++;
    buffer->timestamp = time(NULL);
    buffer->statusFlag = 0;
}

RX_BUFFER* allocateBuffer() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (numBuffers >= MAX_BUFFERS) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return NULL;
    }

    RX_BUFFER* buffer = malloc(sizeof(RX_BUFFER));
    initializeBuffer(buffer);

    buffers[numBuffers++] = buffer;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return buffer;
}

void freeBuffer(int bufferID) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < numBuffers; ++i) {
        if (buffers[i]->bufferID == bufferID) {
            free(buffers[i]);

            buffers[i] = buffers[--numBuffers];

            break;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code uses a simple array to manage the pool of RX_BUFFER structures. When a buffer is allocated, it's added to the end of the array and its index in the array is used as its ID. This allows for efficient allocation and deallocation of buffers, but does require that all buffers are freed before the program exits.

The `allocateBuffer` function uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when allocating or freeing buffers. It also checks if there's room in the pool before attempting to allocate a new buffer, and returns NULL if there isn't.

This code doesn't include any mechanism for tracking buffer usage and availability, reallocating buffers when needed due to traffic fluctuations, or integrating with an external configuration API. Those would be significant additions that would depend on the specific requirements of your project. 

The memory allocation strategy used here is simple dynamic memory allocation using `malloc` and `free`. This can be a good choice for many applications, but it may not be optimal for all situations. For example, if you know in advance how many buffers you'll need, you could use a fixed-size pool of buffers to avoid the overhead of dynamic memory allocation. Or if your application has specific memory usage requirements that can't be met by dynamic allocation, you might need to consider using a different strategy such as custom memory pools or arena allocators.
