```c
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char* appendSlashIfAppropriate(const char* input, int sizeLimit, char* buffer) {
    if (input == NULL || buffer == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    // Check if the string length is within limit and there's enough space in the buffer for a slash.
    int inputLength = strlen(input);
    if (inputLength >= sizeLimit || inputLength + 2 > strlen(buffer)) {
        return NULL;
    }

    // Copy the string to the buffer and append a slash.
    strcpy(buffer, input);
    buffer[inputLength] = '/';
    buffer[inputLength + 1] = '\0';  // Null-terminate the string.

    return buffer;
}
```
This function first checks if either of the pointers is null and returns NULL in such cases. It then checks if the input string length is within the specified limit and there's enough space for a slash at the end of the string in the provided buffer. If these conditions are met, it copies the input string to the buffer and appends a slash. Otherwise, it returns NULL.

