['Here is an example of how you might structure this in Java using Guava's CacheBuilder, which provides a simple way of adding caching to your application. For logging, we will use SLF4J with Logback for implementation. 

```java
import com.google.common.cache.Cache;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class HashingComponent {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(HashingComponent.class);

    // Cache for storing hashed messages
    private final Cache<String, byte[]> cache;
    private MessageDigest messageDigest;

    public HashingComponent() {
        this("SHA-256");
    }

    public HashingComponent(String algorithm) {
        try {
            messageDigest = MessageDigest.getInstance(algorithm);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("Error initializing hashing component", e);
        }

        // Initialize cache with a maximum size and expire time
        this.cache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(10_000) 
                .expireAfterWrite(2, TimeUnit.MINUTES) 
                .build();
    }

    public synchronized byte[] hashMessage(String message) {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        // Check if the message is in cache
        byte[] hashedMessage = cache.getIfPresent(message);
        if (hashedMessage != null) {
            logger.info("Cache hit for message: {}", message);
            return hashedMessage;
        }

        // If not, compute the hash and store it in cache
        try {
            byte[] input = message.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
            hashedMessage = messageDigest.digest(input);

            logger.info("Computed hash for message: {}", message);
            cache.put(message, hashedMessage);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("Error computing hash for message: " + message, e);
        }

        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        logger.info("Time taken to compute hash: {} ms", endTime - startTime);

        return hashedMessage;
    }

    public void setAlgorithm(String algorithm) {
        try {
            messageDigest = MessageDigest.getInstance(algorithm);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("Error setting hashing algorithm", e);
        }

        // Clear cache when changing the algorithm
        cache.invalidateAll();
    }
}
```
This is a simple example and does not include all the requirements you mentioned, such as integration with external data sources or dynamic configuration changes for hashing algorithm selection. You would need to add these features yourself based on your specific needs. 

Also note that this code uses synchronized methods which can lead to performance issues in high-concurrency scenarios. For a more scalable solution, you might want to consider using a lock-free concurrent data structure or an actor model for managing dependencies between different hashing operations and external services.
